While the aforementioned methods can be used to compute weights dynamically based on the loss gradients, a less-investigated issue for the stochastic optimization of finite-sum multi-objective problem is how we should order the samples for the computation of their stochastic gradients so that problem (1) can be solved efficiently. For single-objective optimization in the finite-sum setting,
