4.3 COMPARISON ON TIME COSTS
The proposed JoGBa has two key steps in each iteration: (i) sample ordering, in which we determine the order of a sample based on its gradients, and (ii) model updating, in which we compute the objective weights and update the model with the weighted gradients. Table 3 compares the time costs of these two steps in each iteration for different multi-objective optimization algorithms on NYUv2 and QM9 data. As can be seen, the time cost of sample ordering is almost negligible compared to that of model update, and is generally the same for the same data set across different multi-objective optimization algorithms. This is intuitive as sample ordering is not related to any specific multi-objective optimization algorithm, and demonstrates that the proposed method does not introduce much additional time cost.
